# Muhsin H. Younis - 7/26/2026
#  VALIDATION INPUT FILE.
#  Note, this is a rather coarse mesh for a problem like this.
#  However this is as good as I can reasonably produce for now,
#  as these calculations are being done on a local machine 
#  rather than an HPC cluster.
#  If interested you may want to use a finer mesh.

PROBLEM GENERAL

MESH
    GMSH       water_tank.msh
    SCALE      30.0 30.0 30.0 # Scales the mesh, as the provided file is actually just 1 x 1 x 1 (with scaled-down field size)
END

MATERIALS
    WATER
        DENSITY 1.0 # g / cc
        H   2
        O   1
    END
END

ANGULAR
    PNSCATTERING    11
END

PARTICLES
    PHOTONS
        GRID        LOGARITHMIC # I have found that using a fine logarithmic grid for photons in this case is very very important
        GROUPS      50          # This is logical because the beam gets sampled at rather low energies, where the photon XS change exponentially
        MIN         0.001
        MAX         5.4
        ANGULAR     SN
        SOLVER      SI
    END
    ELECTRONS
        GRID        LOGARITHMIC
        GROUPS      50
        MIN         0.001
        MAX         5.4
        ANGULAR     SN
        SOLVER      GMRES
    END
END

BEAM
    PARTICLE 1
    POLYCHROMATIC 6MV_photons.txt
    AXIS    0.0  0.0 -1.0   # Defined in the mesh coordinate system (after any mesh scaling/translating)
    ORIGIN  0.0  0.0  130.0
    CUTOUT  RECTANGLE 100.0 0.0 10.0 10.0
    SPHERICAL
END

POSTPROCESSING
    FLUENCE
    ENERGY
    DOSE
    CHARGE
END

STORAGELIMITED # I'm doing this to avoid a large h5 file. Consider turning this off for your own run
